"[2510.11683v1] Boundary-Guided Policy Optimization for Memory-efficient RL of Diffusion Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11683v1)\nciteturn2793view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11683v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 29 May 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:Boundary-Guided Policy Optimization for Memory-efficient RL of Diffusion Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Nianyi Lin , cite7†Jiajie Zhang , cite8†Lei Hou , cite9†Juanzi Li L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Boundary-Guided Policy Optimization for Memory-efficient RL of Diffusion Large Language Models, by Nianyi Lin and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:A key challenge in applying reinforcement learning (RL) to diffusion large language models (dLLMs) lies in the intractability of their likelihood functions, which are essential for the RL objective, necessitating corresponding approximation in each training step.\nL17: While existing methods approximate the log-likelihoods by their evidence lower bounds (ELBOs) via customized Monte Carlo (MC) sampling, the forward computational graphs of all MC samples need to be retained for the gradient computation of non-linear terms in the RL objective, resulting in significant memory overhead. This constraint restricts feasible sample sizes, leading to imprecise likelihood approximations and ultimately distorting the RL objective.\nL18: To overcome this limitation, we propose \\emph{Boundary-Guided Policy Optimization} (BGPO), a memory-efficient RL algorithm that maximizes a specially constructed lower bound of the ELBO-based objective.\nL19: This lower bound is carefully designed to satisfy two key properties: (1) Linearity: it is formulated in a linear sum where each term depends only on a single MC sample, thereby enabling gradient accumulation across samples and ensuring constant memory usage; (2) Equivalence: Both the value and gradient of this lower bound are equal to those of the ELBO-based objective in on-policy training, making it also an effective approximation for the original RL objective.\nL20: These properties allow BGPO to adopt a large MC sample size, resulting in more accurate likelihood approximations and improved RL objective estimation, which in turn leads to enhanced performance. Experiments show that BGPO significantly outperforms previous RL algorithms for dLLMs in math problem solving, code generation, and planning tasks.\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2510.11683 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2510.11683v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11683†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Nianyi Lin [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 17:47:50 UTC (234 KB)\nL29: cite16†[v2] Tue, 14 Oct 2025 09:26:10 UTC (234 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v3] Fri, 29 May 2026 17:51:48 UTC (230 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled Boundary-Guided Policy Optimization for Memory-efficient RL of Diffusion Large Language Models, by Nianyi Lin and 3 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite10†View PDF L39:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite17†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite18†view license L43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.LG\nL46: \nL47: cite19†< prev |   cite2