sibility†info.arxiv.org · cite57†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L167: \nL168: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.11690v1] Diffusion Transformers with Representation Autoencoders (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11690v1)\nciteturn2793view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11690v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:Diffusion Transformers with Representation Autoencoders\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Boyang Zheng , cite6†Nanye Ma , cite7†Shengbang Tong , cite8†Saining Xie L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Diffusion Transformers with Representation Autoencoders, by Boyang Zheng and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Latent generative modeling, where a pretrained autoencoder maps pixels into a latent space for the diffusion process, has become the standard strategy for Diffusion Transformers (DiT); however, the autoencoder component has barely evolved.\nL17: Most DiTs continue to rely on the original VAE encoder, which introduces several limitations: outdated backbones that compromise architectural simplicity, low-dimensional latent spaces that restrict information capacity, and weak representations that result from purely reconstruction-based training and ultimately limit generative quality.\nL18: In this work, we explore replacing the VAE with pretrained representation encoders (e.g., DINO, SigLIP, MAE) paired with trained decoders, forming what we term Representation Autoencoders (RAEs). These models provide both high-quality reconstructions and semantically rich latent spaces, while allowing for a scalable transformer-based architecture. Since these latent spaces are typically high-dimensional, a key challenge is enabling diffusion transformers to operate effectively within them.\nL19: We analyze the sources of this difficulty, propose theoretically motivated solutions, and validate them empirically. Our approach achieves faster convergence without auxiliary representation alignment losses. Using a DiT variant equipped with a lightweight, wide DDT head, we achieve strong image generation results on ImageNet: 1.51 FID at 256x256 (no guidance) and 1.13 at both 256x256 and 512x512 (with guidance). RAE offers clear advantages and should be the new default for diffusion transformer training.\nL20: Comments:  | Technical Report; Project Page: cite11†this https URL†rae-dit.github.io L21: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2510.11690 [cs.CV]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2510.11690v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11690†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Nanye Ma [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 17:51:39 UTC (14,952 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Diffusion Transformers with Representation Autoencoders, by Boyang Zheng and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CV\nL44: \nL45: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L46: \nL47: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-10 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite23†cs L52: cite24†cs